For Part II, let  $ax+by+cz=d$  be a plane that cuts the surface  $xy+yz+xz=0$  (i).  Substituting  $z = \frac{d-ax-by}{c}$  into our surface equation yields: $xy + z(x+y) = xy + (x+y)\left(\frac{d-ax-by}{c}\right) = \frac{(c-a-b)xy+dx+dy-ax^2-by^2}{c} = 0$ ;

or  $ax^2+(a+b-c)xy+by^2-dx-dy=0$  (ii); 

of which (ii) fits the standard conic quadratic form  $Ax^2+Bxy+Cy^2+Dx+Ey+F=0$  (iii).  

If our proposed plane cuts (i) in circles, then we require  $A=C, B=0$  from (iii)  $\Rightarrow \textcolor{red}{a=b=t, c= 2t}$  (for  $t \in \mathbb{R}$ ) in (ii). 

If our proposed plane cuts (i) in parabolas, then we require  $B^2-4AC = 0$  from (iii), or: $(a+b-c)^2-4ab = 0 \Rightarrow (a-b)^2 - 2(a+b)c+ c^2=0 \Rightarrow c = \frac{2(a+b)\pm \sqrt{4(a+b)^2-4(a-b)^2}}{2}$ ;

or  $\textcolor{red}{c = (a+b) \pm2\sqrt{ab}}$  (for real  $ab \ge 0$ ).


Please feel free to ping me if you have a solution to Part I!

