Consider using iteration (which is using the definition of something on itself over and over, for example if  $f(x)=(1+x)f(x^2)$  then  $f(x)=(1+x)(1+x^2)f(x^4)$  and so on...)


If
\begin{align*}
\sin(x)\approx\left(\frac{1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right)}{3}\right) \sin\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)
\end{align*}
then this would iterate into something very close to what we want.  Based on that, what we actually need is
\begin{align*}
\sin(x)=\left(1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right)\right) \sin\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)~~~~~\mbox{or equivilently}~~~~~\sin(3x)=\left(1+2\cos\left(2x\right)\right) \sin\left(x\right)
\end{align*}
And sure enough
\begin{align*}
\sin(3x)=\frac{e^{3\pi i} - e^{-3\pi i}}{2i}=\left(1+2\left(\frac{e^{2\pi i} + e^{-2\pi i}}{2}\right)\right)\frac{e^{\pi i} - e^{-\pi i}}{2i}=\left(1+2\cos\left(2x\right)\right) \sin\left(x\right)
\end{align*}
Doing the iteration we have
\begin{align*}
\sin(x)&=\left(1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right)\right) \sin\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)
&=\left(1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right)\right) \left(1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3^2}\right)\right) \sin\left(\frac{x}{3^2}\right)
&\vdots
&=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left(\prod_{k=1}^{n} 1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3^{k}}\right)\right) \sin\left(\frac{x}{3^n}\right)
&=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left(\prod_{k=1}^{n} \frac{1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3^{k}}\right)}{3}\right) 3^n\sin\left(\frac{x}{3^n}\right)
&=\left(\prod_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3^{k}}\right)}{3}\right)x
\end{align*}
where the last step uses the fact that  $\lim_{\theta\rightarrow0} \frac{\theta}{\sin(\theta)} = 1$ .




***Remark 1:**  Instead of approaching this with an iteration mindset, you could approach it with a telescoping mindset and say if  $\frac{\sin(x)}{\sin\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)}=\left(1+2\cos\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right)\right)$  then the product would telescope into the answer.*



***Remark 2:**  If you like suffering, it should be possible to show that, after taking the natural log of each side, both sides have the same power series coefficients.  You could use the power series for  $\ln(1+x)$  with  $x=\cos(\theta)$  combined with the fact that  $\cos^n \theta = \frac{1}{2^n}\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}e^{i(2k-n)\theta}$  followed by a bunch of combinatorial sums.*

