A closed plane curve  $K$  for a set of points  $K \in \{P_1, P_2, \dots, P_n\}$  will be closed for a minimum of  $3$  points. Consider placing  $3$  distinct points  $P_1, P_2,$  and  $P_3$  who are a distance of  $1$  from the other two points, or an equilateral triangle. If we want to add more points to this equilateral triangle, we must have a condition that the distance from all two points in  $K$  will be less than  $1$ . Draw a circle  $C_1$  of radius  $1$  from  $P_1$  and do similarly for  $P_2$  and  $P_3$  as well. 
[asy]
draw((0, 0) -- (1, 0) -- (1/2, sqrt(3)/2) -- cycle);
draw(circle((0, 0), 1));
draw(circle((1, 0), 1));
draw(circle((1/2, sqrt(3)/2), 1));
[/asy]
Each circle is the set of all areas that a point  $P_n$  can be placed such that it is a distance from one of the points. For our condition to be satisfied, the region of all points must be in the intersection of all three circles, or  $C_1 \cap C_2 \cap C_3$ . It is thus apparent that the greatest distance from the center of the triangle is its circumradius, or  $r = 1/\sqrt{3}$ . This circle will encapsulate all points in  $K$ .

