Let  $S_n$  denote the set of all permutations on  $[n]=\{1,\ldots,n\}$ . Then the indicated matrix takes the form  $[1 - \mathbf{1}_{\{i=j\}}]_{i,j\in[n]}$ , and so, the desired number is
\begin{align*}
\sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \prod_{i\in[n]} (1 - \mathbf{1}_{\{\sigma(i) = i\}})
&= \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \sum_{I \subseteq [n]} (-1)^{|I|} \prod_{i\in I} \mathbf{1}_{\{\sigma(i) = i\}} 
&= \sum_{I \subseteq [n]} (-1)^{|I|} \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \prod_{i\in I} \mathbf{1}_{\{\sigma(i) = i\}} 
&= \sum_{I \subseteq [n]} (-1)^{|I|} \text{[\# of permutations on  $[n]\setminus I$ ]} 
&= \sum_{I \subseteq [n]} (-1)^{|I|} (n - |I|)! 
&= \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k \binom{n}{k}(n-k)! 
&= n! \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}
\end{align*}
Note that this counts the number of fixed-point free permutations.

