Since  $n$  is odd, we can express  $n$  as  $2k+1$  where  $k$  is some positive integer. Assume for the purposes of induction that  $x^{j} - \dfrac1{x^{j}}$  is expressible by  $x+\dfrac1x$  for all  $j\le 2k-1$ . Expanding, we have
\begin{align*}
\left(x - \dfrac1x \right)^{2k+1} &= \dbinom{2k+1}{2k+1} x^{2k+1} + \dbinom{2k+1}{2k} x^{2k} \left( -\dfrac1x \right) + \dotsb + \dbinom{2k+1}{1} x\left( -\dfrac1x \right)^{2k} + \dbinom{2k+1}{0} \left(-\dfrac1x\right)^{2k+1} 
&= \dbinom{2k+1}{2k+1}x^{2k+1} + (-1)^{2k} \dbinom{2k+1}{2k}x^{2k-1} + \dotsb + (-1)^{1} \dbinom{2k+1}{1} \left( \dfrac1x \right)^{2k-1} + (-1)^{0}\dbinom{2k+1}{0} \left(\dfrac1x\right)^{2k+1} 
&= \left(x^{2k+1} - \dfrac{1}{x^{2k+1}}\right) + (-1)^{2k} \dbinom{2k+1}{2k}x^{2k-1} + \dotsb + (-1)^{1} \dbinom{2k+1}{1} \left( \dfrac1x \right)^{2k-1}.
\end{align*}

Since everything after the first term is expressible by assumption, we have  $x^n -\dfrac1{x^n}$  is expressible by  $x-\dfrac1x$ .  $\square$

