or  $x = \pm \frac{a^2}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}$  and  $y = \pm \frac{b^2}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}$  (iii).

Next, the four tangency points form an inscribed rectangle (with sides parallel to the coordinate axes).  For any rectangle of side lengths  $x$  and  $y$  & given perimeter  $P$  (i.e.  $P = 2x + 2y$ ), the maximum area is attained is: $A(x) = xy = x(P/2 - x) = -x^2 + Px/2 = -(x - P/4)^2 + (P^2)/16  \Rightarrow x = y = P/4$  

or when the rectangle is a square.  If the maximum rectangle formed by the above tangency points has side lengths: $\frac{2a^2}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}} = \frac{2b^2}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}  \Rightarrow  a = b$  (iv)

