Because  $f(x)=(3x+1)(x+2)$ , we naturally guess the answer should be  $g(x)=(3x+1)(2x+1)$ .
We calculate  $$  \Gamma(f(x)^n)=\sum_{p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2, \atop 0\le p_i ,q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{2n-q_1-q_2} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose q_1}{n\choose q_2}, $$    $$  \Gamma(g(x)^n)=\sum_{p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2, \atop 0\le p_i ,q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{q_1+q_2} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose q_1}{n\choose q_2}. $$  We see that  $\sum_{p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2, \atop 0\le p_i ,q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{2n-q_1-q_2} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose q_1}{n\choose q_2}=\sum_{p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2, \atop 0\le p_i ,q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{(n-q_1)+(n-q_2)} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose (n-q_1)}{n\choose (n-q_2)}
=\sum_{p_1+n-q_2=p_2+n-q_1, \atop 0\le p_i ,n-q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{(n-q_1)+(n-q_2)} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose (n-q_1)}{n\choose (n-q_2)}=\sum_{p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2, \atop 0\le p_i ,q_i\le n, i=1,2} 3^{p_1+p_2} 2^{q_1+q_2} {n \choose p_1}{n\choose p_2}{n \choose q_1}{n\choose q_2},$ and thus  $\Gamma(f(x)^n)=\Gamma(g(x)^n)$ .

