The <span style="color:#f00">**<u>[u]solution</u>[/u]**</span>:
From the conditions we can say that  $P(x)=\prod_1^n (x-r_j)$ . Differentiating  $P$  we have  $P'(x)= P(x) \sum_1^n \frac{1}{x-r_j}$ . Now we put  $x=\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}$ .(Here we assume  $n\ge 3$ )Then   $$ 0=P'\left(\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}\right)=P\left(\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}\right)\left(\sum_1^{n-2}\frac{1}{\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}-r_j} + \cancel{\frac{2}{r_n-r_{n-1}}}+\cancel{\frac{2}{-r_n+r_{n-1}}}\right) $$   However we note that the sum is strictly  $>0$  due to the ordering of the roots(further such a sum will appear as  $n-2\ge 1$ ). Also  $r_{n-1}<\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}<r_n$ . Thus  $P\left(\frac{r_{n-1}+r_n}{2}\right) \neq 0$ (Contradiction!) Thus  $n=2$  is only left and it's easy to check that all real quadratic polynomials with two distinct real roots satisfy the conditions.  \blacksquare

