Consider the function  $g(x)=f(x)-\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ . Clearly  $g$  is infinitely many times differentiable and further we are interested in values around  $0$ .
Note that  $g(\frac{1}{n})=0 \implies \text{ by continuity }g(0)=0$ The Claim is that  $g^{(k)}(0)=0$ . We use induction on  $k$  to prove this. Base case, that is,  $k=0$  is already proven. Now suppose  $g^{(n)}(0)=0$  for all  $0\le n \le K$ . By Taylor Theorem, for all  $x\in (-1,1)$ ,   $$ g(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{K} \frac{g^{(i)}(0)}{i!}x^i + \frac{g^{(K+1)}(c_x)}{(k+1)!}x^{K+1}\stackrel{\text{Induct}}{=} \frac{g^{(K+1)}(c_x)}{(k+1)!}x^{K+1} $$  for some  $c_x \in (0,x)$ .
Hence  $g^{(K+1)}(c_{1/n}) = 0$  on putting  $x=1/n$ . As  $g$  is infinitely many times differentiable, we can use continuity on the  $(K+1)$ th derivative to get  $g^{(K+1)}(0)=0$ (Note that as  $|c_{1/n}| < 1/n$ , Squeeze theorem implies  $\lim_{n\to \infty} c_{1/n} = 0$ ). This completes the Induction step.
Finally using  $f^{(k)}(0)=\left(\frac{1}{1+x^2}\right)^{(k)}_{x=0}$ , we can immediately conclude that \[f^{(k)}(0) = \left\{ 
\begin{array}{cl}
0 & \text{if k is odd,} 
k! & \text{if k } \equiv 0 \pmod 4, 
 -k! &  \text{if k } \equiv 2 \pmod 4. 
\end{array} \right.\]  $\blacksquare$

