**Motivation**: In order for a straight line to intersect this graph at  $4$  distinct points, we need to choose a sufficient  $c$  such that the graph has two local minima and a single local maximum.  Choosing such a  $c$  will result in a  $4$  point intersection of a single straight line, as desired.**Solution**: Notice that we have  $$ f(x)=x^4 + 9x^3 + cx^2 + 9x + 4\implies f'(x)=4x^3+27x^2+2cx+9. $$  From Descartes' Rule of Signs we have  $$ f'(x)=4x^3+27x^2+2cx+9\implies f'(-x)=-4x^3+27x^2-2cx+9 $$  which means the derivative of  $f(x)$  can have at most  $3$  real roots.  We need to find such a  $c$  where this is true.  In order for  $f'(x)$  to have  $3$  real roots, we need  $f''(x)$  to have two real roots so that it has one minimum and one maximum.  To wit, we need sufficient values of  $c$  such that  $$ 12x^2+54x+2c=0. $$  Using the quadratic formula we get
\begin{align*}
x&=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}
&=\frac{-54\pm\sqrt{54^2-4(12)(2c)}}{2(12)}
&=\frac{-54\pm\sqrt{2916-96c}}{24}.
\end{align*}
Notice that we must have  $2916-96c> 0\implies \boxed{c<\frac{243}{8}}$  since our two solutions must be real.  We can confirm that this bound on  $c$  yields the desired condition by testing it, and doing so we find that this is the correct bound and we are done.

