&=\sqrt{x}\left(1+\frac a{2x}+O(x^{-2})\right)
&=x^{1/2}+\frac a2x^{-1/2}+O(x^{-3/2})\end{align*}
Hence,  $\sqrt{x+a}-\sqrt{x}=\frac a2x^{-3/2}+O(x^{-5/2}).$ Now, take the square root of that:
\begin{align*}\sqrt{\sqrt{x+a}-\sqrt{x}}&=\left(\frac a2x^{-1/2}+O(x^{-3/2}\right)^{\frac12}
&=\left(\frac a2x^{-1/2}\right)^{\frac12}\left(1+O(x^{-1})\right)^{\frac12}
&=\sqrt{\frac a2}x^{-1/4}+O(x^{-5/4})\end{align*}
In a very similar fashion,
\begin{align*}\sqrt{\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{x-b}}=\sqrt{\frac b2}x^{-1/4}+O(x^{-5/4})\end{align*}
Which means that
