Rewrite
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{\left\lfloor\frac{2p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{k}=2^{p}-\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{k}-1
\end{align*}
And
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{k}&=\left[x^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]\frac{1}{1-x}(1+x)^p
&=\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]\frac{1}{1-x^3}(1+x^3)^p\tag{1}
&=\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]\frac{1}{1-x^3}(1+x)^p(\omega+x)^p(\omega^2+x)^p
&\equiv\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]\frac{1}{1-x^3}\left((1+x)^p(\omega^p+x^p)(\omega^{2p}+x^p)+(\omega+x)^p(1+x^p)(\omega^{2p}+x^p)+(\omega^2+x)^p(1+x^p)(\omega^p+x^p)-2(1+x^p)(\omega^p+x^p)(\omega^{2p}+x^p)\right)\mod p^2\tag{2}
&=\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]\frac{1}{1-x^3}\left((1+x)^p+(\omega+x)^p\omega^{2p}+(\omega^2+x)^p\omega^p-2\right)\tag{3}
&=\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{3k}+\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{3k}\omega^{p-3k}\omega^{2p}+\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{3k}(\omega^2)^{p-3k}\omega^p-2\tag{4}
&=3\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{3k}-2
&=3\left(\frac{(1+1)^p+(1+\omega)^p+(1+\omega^2)^p}{3}\right)-2\tag{5}
&=3\left(\frac{2^p+(1+\omega)^p+\overline{(1+\omega)^p}}{3}\right)-2
&=3\left(\frac{2^p+2\operatorname{Re}\left(e^{\frac{p i \pi}{3}}\right)}{3}\right)-2\tag{6}
&=3\left(\frac{2^p+1}{3}\right)-2
&=2^p-1
\end{align*}
Plugging this result into the equation at the top of this proof gives
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{\left\lfloor\frac{2p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{k}=2^{p}-\sum_{k=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\binom{p}{k}-1\equiv 2^p-(2^p-1)-1=0\mod p^2
\end{align*}
which completes the proof.



To understand  $\textrm{(1)}$  note that replacing  $x$  by  $x^3$  just moves the coefficient we are tracking to a different power of  $x$ .

To understand  $\textrm{(2)}$  note that when two polynomials or power series are considered congruent modulo  $m$ , it means their corresponding coefficients are congruent modulo  $m$ .  For example  $x\not\equiv x^2 \mod 2$  as polynomials even though  $n\equiv n^2\mod 2$  as integers.  The coefficients of the expanded binomials have a factor of  $p$  for the interior terms but no factor of  $p$  for the edge terms.  If two interior terms are multiplied together their product will have a coefficient of  $0 \mod p^2$ .  So we only need to consider cross terms that have one or fewer interior terms.  The expression  $(1+x)^p(\omega^p+x^p)(\omega^{2p}+x^p)$  contains all terms (both interior and edge terms) of  $(1+x)^p$  but only the edge terms of  $(\omega+x)^p$  and  $(\omega^2+x)^p$ .  When we add the three similar expressions together we get three copies of the cross terms made up of only edge terms (an over count of 2 each) so we subtract  $2(1+x^p)(\omega^p+x^p)(\omega^{2p}+x^p)$ .

To understand  $\textrm{(3)}$  note that the powers of  $x^p$  are too large to make a contribution to  $\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]$  so we can remove them (unless  $p=3$  but the problem statement explicitly says  $p$  is greater than  $3$ ).  And also use the fact  $\omega^3=1$ .

To understand  $\textrm{(4)}$  note that all the  $0\mod 3$  powers of  $x$  lower that  $x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}$  contribute to  $\left[x^{3\left\lfloor\frac{p}{3}\right\rfloor}\right]$  because they get bumped up by the factor of  $\frac{1}{1-x^3}$ .  

To understand  $\textrm{(5)}$  this is a standard application of the roots of unity filter.

To understand  $\textrm{(6)}$  note that  $e^{\frac{i \pi}{3}}$  is  $1/6$ th of the way around the complex unit circle (halfway around divided by 3 is  $1/6$ th of the way around).  Since  $p$  is always  $1$  or  $5 \mod 6$  then  $e^{\frac{p i \pi}{3}}$  is always on the positive real side of the complex unit circle with real part  $\frac{1}{2}$ .

