Connecting the dots between my conjectures and jmerrry's solution.

Using the notation in my post above, jmerry says that \[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}y(n,k+1,j)z^k=(1+z)^{n-j}(1-z)^{j-1}.\]
Now multiply by  $z$  and relabel: \begin{align*}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}y(n,k+1,j)z^{k+1}&=z(1+z)^{n-j}(1-z)^{j-1}
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}y(n,k,j)z^{k}&=z(1+z)^{n-j}(1-z)^{j-1}\end{align*}
But since  $y(n,0,j)=0,$  we can go ahead and start that last sum at  $k=0.$  Then we add the two sums together to get \begin{align*}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}(y(n,k+1,j)+y(n,k,j))z^k&=(1+z)^{n+1-j}(1-z)^{j-1}
&=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}y(n+1,k+1,j)z^k\end{align*}
Equate coefficients of the same powers, and we have the Pascal recursion I wanted. And everything else I claimed follows from this structure.

