We compute the determinant of  $I+A$  by computing its eigenvalues. The eigenvalues of  $I+A$  are obtained by adding 1 to each of the eigenvalues of  $A,$  so
it suffices to compute the latter. We claim that the eigenvalues of  $A$  are  $n / 2,-n / 2,0, \ldots ., 0,$  where 0 occurs with multiplicity  $n-2$ . To prove this claim, define vectors  $v^{(m)}, 0 \leq m \leq n-1$  componentwise by  $\left.\left(v^{(m)}\right)_{k}=e^{i k m \theta} \text { (where } \theta=2 \pi / n\right)$ . If we form a matrix from the  $v^{(m)},$  its determinant is a Vandermonde product and hence is nonzero. (See  $1986 \mathrm{A} 6$  for a short explanation of the Vandermonde determinant.) Thus the  $v^{(m)}$  form a basis for  $C^{n} .$  since  $\cos z=\left(e^{i z}+e^{-i z}\right) / 2$  for any  $z$   $$ \begin{aligned}
\left(A v^{(m)}\right)_{j} &=\sum_{k=1}^{n} \cos (j \theta+k \theta) e^{i k m \theta} 
&=\frac{e^{i j \theta}}{2} \sum_{k=1}^{n} e^{i k(m+1) \theta}+\frac{e^{-i j \theta}}{2} \sum_{k=1}^{n} e^{i k(m-1) \theta}
\end{aligned} $$  since  $\sum_{k=1}^{n} e^{i k t \theta}=0$  for integer  $\ell$  unless  $n | \ell,$  we conclude that  $A v^{(m)}=0$  for  $m=0$  and for  $2 \leq m \leq n-1$ . In addition, we find that  $\left(A v^{(1)}\right) j=\frac{n}{2} e^{-10 \theta}=\frac{n}{2}\left(v^{(n-1)}\right)_{j}$ and  $\left(A v^{(n-1)}\right)_{j}=\frac{n}{2} e^{i j \theta}=\frac{n}{2}\left(v^{(1)}\right)_{j},$  so  $A\left(v^{(1)} \pm v^{(n-1)}\right)=\pm \frac{n}{2}\left(v^{(1)} \pm v^{(n-1)}\right) .$  Thus $\left\{v^{(0)}, v^{(2)}, v^{(3)}, \ldots, v^{(n-2)}, v^{(1)}+v^{(n-1)}, v^{(1)}-v^{(n-1)}\right\}$  is a basis for  $C^{n}$  of eigenvectors
of  $A$  with the claimed eigenvalues. Finally, the determinant of  $I+A$  is the product of  $(1+\lambda)$  over all eigenvalues  $\lambda$  of $A,$  namely,  $$ \operatorname{det}(I+A)=(1+n / 2)(1-n / 2)=1-n^{2} / 4 $$  Source:ks kedlaya Putnam 1985-2000

Another Putnam integral off my bucket list, solved with a small hint :D

The key observation is that  $\sin(x)\sin(x^2)=\frac{1}{2}\cos(x - x^2) - \frac{1}{2}\cos(x^2 + x)$ .  Breaking apart the integrand we have  $$ \lim_{B \rightarrow \infty} \int_{0}^{B} \sin (x) \sin (x^2) dx\implies \lim_{B \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{B}\cos(x - x^2) \,dx-\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{B}\cos(x^2 + x)\,dx. $$  We choose the second integral to evaluate.  To motivate a future substitution, notice that  $\frac{d}{dx} x^2+1=2x+1$ .  We can manipulate the integrand like so
\begin{align*}
\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{B}\cos(x^2 + x)\,dx&=\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{B}\frac{2x+1}{2x+1}\cos(x^2 + x)\,dx.
\end{align*}
To cancel things out, we will use integration by parts by letting  $u=\frac{1}{2x+1}$  with  $du=-\frac{2}{(2 x+1)^2}\,dx$  and  $dv=(2x+1)\cos(x^2+x)\,dx$  with  $v=\sin(x^2 + x)$ .  This means
\begin{align*}
\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty} \int_{0}^{B}\frac{2x+1}{2x+1}\cos(x^2 + x)\,dx&=\lim_{B\to\infty}\left(\frac{\sin(x^2 + x)}{2x+1}\right)\biggr|_0^B+\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{B}\frac{2\sin(x^2 + x)}{(2x+1)^2}\,dx.
\end{align*}
We can first determine the convergence of the first limit.  We have  $$ \left(\frac{\sin(x^2 + x)}{2x+1}\right)\biggr|_0^B=\lim_{B\to\infty}\left(\frac{\sin(B^2 + B)}{2B+1}\right)=0. $$  The convergence of  $\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2\sin(x^2 + x)}{(2x+1)^2}\,dx$  is evident by comparison of  $\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2}{(2x+1)^2}\,dx$ , and since we have  $\frac{2\sin(x^2 + x)}{(2x+1)^2}\le \frac{2}{(2x+1)^2}$  for all  $x\in[0,\infty]$  and  $\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2}{(2x+1)^2}\,dx$  converges, it follows that  $\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2\sin(x^2 + x)}{(2x+1)^2}\,dx$  must converge as well.

The argument for the first integral,  $\int_{0}^{B}\cos(x - x^2) \,dx$ , follows the same argument used for the second which we already performed.  Since both integrals converge, this means that  $\lim_{B \rightarrow \infty} \int_{0}^{B} \sin (x) \sin (x^2) dx$  must converge as well which finishes  $\square$

