Denote by  $d$  the length of the longest side of the triangle. Since the distance between two integer lattice points is always at least 1, we must have  $d \ge 1$  and hence always  $d \ge r^{1/3}$  for  $r \le 1.$  So WLOG we may assume  $r > 1.$  A triangle inscribed in the circle either encloses the center of the circle or it does not. If the triangle does enclose the center of the circle, then its longest side must as a chord subtend an angle at least   $\frac{2\pi}3$   (but no more than  $\pi$ )  and have length at least the chord of   $\frac{2\pi}3$    in a circle of radius  $r.$  That is,  $d \ge \sqrt{3}\, r > r > r^{1/3}$  since  $r > 1.$  This leaves the case in which the triangle does not enclose the center of the circle.

In that case, the triangle has base  $d$  and altitude no more then the greatest distance (away from the center) from this chord to the circle. That is,
\begin{align*}\text{(altitude)}\le r-\sqrt{r^2-\frac{d^2}4}&=\frac{\left(r-\sqrt{r^2-\frac{d^2}4}\right)\left(r+\sqrt{r^2-\frac{d^2}4}\right)}{r+\sqrt{r^2-\frac{d^2}{4r}}}
&\le\frac{\frac{d^2}4}{r+\sqrt{r^2-\frac{d^2}4}}\le\frac{d^2}{4r}.\end{align*}
Let  $A$  be the area of the triangle. Then  $A \le  \frac12  d\cdot \frac{d^2}{4r}   \le  \frac{d^3}{8r}  .$  But Pick’s Theorem constrains the area of the triangle. Pick’s Theorem states that the area of a polygon whose vertices are integer lattice points equals (# of lattice points in interior)  $+  \frac12$  (# of lattice points on boundary)   $-1.$  This triangle has at least 3 lattice points on its boundary, so  $A \ge  \frac12 $ . So,   $\frac12   \le A \le  \frac{d^3}{8r}   ,$  or  $d^3 \ge 4r > r,$  so  $d \ge r^{1/3}.$

