We forget that area of triangle  $\triangle ABC$  was given and will compute the ratios of areas of  $\triangle ABC, \triangle RST$ . We may Wlog assume  $\triangle ABC$  is equilateral (by means of a Affine Transformation) and  $BC = CA =AB = \sqrt{5} + 1$  (since at the end of the day, we have to find the ratio of the areas of the triangles  $\triangle ABC, \triangle RST$ ). 

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim 1:**</span>  $AG = BE = CF = \sqrt{5} - 1$  and  $BG = CE = AF = 2$ .
*proof:* WLOG assume that  $AG = \min(AG,BE,CF)$  which also means  $BG = \max(BG,CE,AF)$ . By Menelaus' Theorem in  $\triangle ABE$  for collinear points  $C,T,G$  and in  $\triangle AGC$  for collinear points  $B,S,F$  gives
\begin{align*}
\frac{GB}{GA} = \frac{CB}{CE} ~~,~~ \frac{BG}{BA} = \frac{FC}{FA}  
\implies ~ \frac{EB}{EC} = \frac{CB}{CE} - 1 = \frac{GB}{GA} -1 \ge \frac{EC}{EB} - 1  ~~ 
\text{and} ~~ \frac{FA}{AC} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{FC}{FA}} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{BG}{BA}}  \le \frac{1}{1 + \frac{FA}{FC}} 
\implies ~ \frac{EB}{EC} \ge \frac{\sqrt{5}-1}{2} ~~,~~ \frac{FA}{AC} \le \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}+1} = \frac{\sqrt{5}-1}{2}
\end{align*}
But By Menelaus' in  $\triangle BFC$  for collinear points  $A,R,E$  gives  $\frac{EB}{EC} = \frac{FA}{AC}$ . But this means equality must hold everywhere, and this implies our desired result.  $\square$ Then using Cosine rule in  $\triangle ABE$  gives  $AE^2 = (\sqrt{5} + 1)^2 + (\sqrt{5} - 1)^2 - (\sqrt{5} + 1)(\sqrt{5} - 1) = 8 ~ \implies AE = 2 \sqrt{2}$ . Now Menelaus's theorem in  $\triangle AEC$  for collinear points  $B,R,F$  gives  $$  1 = \frac{BE}{BC} \cdot \frac{FC}{FA} \cdot \frac{RA}{RE} = \frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{\sqrt{5} + 1} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{2} \cdot \frac{RA}{RE} = (\sqrt{5} - 2) \cdot \frac{RA}{RE}  $$  This gives  $RE = \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{2}}$ . Also,  $TE = \sqrt{2}$ . Thus,  $$ TR = TE - RE = \sqrt{2} - \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{\sqrt{2}} $$  Similarly, we obtain that  $RS = ST = \frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{2}$ . So both the triangles  $\triangle ABC, \triangle RST$  are equilateral and thus  $$ \frac{[RST]}{[ABC]} = \left(\frac{\frac{\sqrt{5} - 1}{\sqrt{2}}}{\sqrt{5} + 1} \right)^2 = \frac{(3 - \sqrt{5})^2}{8} = \boxed{\frac{7 - 3 \sqrt{5}}{4}} \to \text{ANSWER}   $$  This completes the proof.  $\blacksquare$

