The picture is not drawn to scale.  $ R$  is the midpoint of  $ \overline{BF},$   $ S$  is the midpoint of  $ \overline{CG},$  and  $ T$  is the midpoint of  $ \overline{AE}.$ Note that nothing was said about the shape of  $ \triangle ABC.$  That means that if the answer is to have any meaning, it must be independent of shape. We note that all of the conditions given for the locations of various points and lines depend only on vector (linear) equations - certain points are midpoints of certain segments, certain points lie at certain intersections. That means that the whole setup is *affine invariant.* That is, if we move the picture by a translation plus a linear transformation, then everything about the locations of points - midpoints, intersections, and so on - remains the same. Furthermore, any affine transformation simply multiplies all areas by the same constant, so the ratio of the area of  $ \triangle RST$  to the area of  $ \triangle ABC$  remains the same - and that ratio is what we are asked to compute.

Since we can transform any possible triangle into any other possible triangle by some affine transformation, we may assume that  $ \triangle ABC$  is any shape we please; in both solutions below, I will eventually assume that  $ \triangle ABC$  is equilateral.

<details><summary>Solution 1: barycentric coordinates.</summary>Suppose we have vectors  $ u,v,w$  such that the coordinates of  $ A$  are given by the vector  $ u$ ,  $ B$  by the vector  $ v,$  and  $ C$  by the vector  $ w.$  The coordinates of any point  $ P$  in  $ \triangle ABC$  can then be written uniquely as a *convex combination* of  $ u,v,w.$  That is, it can be written as  $ \alpha u+\beta v+\gamma w$  such that  $ \alpha,\beta,\gamma\ge 0$  and  $ \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1.$  The ordered triple  $ (\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$  is then the barycentric coordinates of  $ P.$  (Note that all the information is contained in  $ \alpha$  and  $ \beta,$  since  $ \gamma=1-\alpha-\beta.$  But the symmetry of writing the triple has its uses.) The barycentric coordinates of points  $ A,B,C$  are  $ (1,0,0),(0,1,0),$  and  $ (0,0,1),$  respectively.

Let  $ E$  have barycentric coordinates  $ (0,s,1-s),$   $ F$  have coordinates  $ (1-t,0,t),$  and  $ G$  have coordinates  $ (u,1-u,0).$  Point  $ T$  is the midpoint of  $ \overline{AE},$  so we average the coordinates of  $ A$  and  $ E$  to get  $ T$  as  $ \left(\frac12,\frac{s}2,\frac{1-s}2\right).$  But  $ T$  lies on  $ \overline{CG},$  which means it has coordinates  $ (xu,x(1-u),1-x)$  for some  $ x\in[0,1].$  Equating these coordinates gives us these equations:
\begin{align*}xu&=\frac12x(1-u)&=\frac{s}21-x&=\frac{1-s}2\end{align*}
Eliminating  $ x$  from these equations leaves us with  $ (1+s)u=1.$ Cycling the same argument around leaves us also with  $ (1+t)s=1$  and  $ (1+u)t=1.$ Solve these equations together.  $ u=\frac1{1+s}.$  Put that in the last equation to get  $ \left(1+\frac1{1+s}\right)t=1$  or  $ t=\frac{1+s}{2+s}.$  Put that into the middle equation to get  $ \left(1+\frac{1+s}{2+s}\right)s=1.$  That becomes the quadratic equation  $ s^2+s-1,$  whose positive root is  $ s=\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}2.$ Again, by cycling the equations around, we get  $ s=t=u=\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}2.$ We then put that into our formula for the barycentric coordinates of  $ T,$  and cycle that around to get the following barycentric coordinates:
\[ R\ \left(\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}4,\frac12,\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}4\right)\]
\[ S\ \left(\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}4,\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}4,\frac12\right)\]
\[ T\ \left(\frac12,\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}4,\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}4\right)\]
Now we need to compute the area. As per the above discussion, we may assume WLOG that  $ \triangle ABC$  is equilateral. It follows that  $ \triangle RST$  is also equilateral. The length of any side of one of our equilateral triangles is proportional the the square root of the sum of the squares of the differences of the barycentric coordinates. To find the area of  $ \triangle RST,$  we need to find the ratio of the squares of the side lengths. For  $ \triangle ABC,$  that's  $ 2.$  For  $ \triangle RST$  we get:
\[ \left(\frac{4-2\sqrt{5}}4\right)^2+\left(\frac{-1+\sqrt{5}}4\right)^2+\left(\frac{-3+\sqrt{5}}4\right)^2=\frac{56-24\sqrt{5}}{16}=\frac{7-3\sqrt{5}}2\]

Divide that by  $ 2$  to get  $ \boxed{\text{area}(\triangle RST)=\frac{7-3\sqrt{5}}{4}.}$</details>

<details><summary>Solution 2:similar triangles</summary>Label sides: $ AB=BC=CD=1.$  $ RS=ST=TR=s.$  (And the final answer for area will be  $ s^2.$ ) $ AT=BR=CS=x.$  $ RE=SF=TG=x-s.$  $ AG=BE=CF=a.$  $ GB=EC=FA=1-a.$ Now we have many similar triangles, notably including that  $ \triangle ATG$  is similar to  $ \triangle ABE$  and  $ \triangle ARF$  is similar to  $ \triangle ACE.$ Find ratios from the similar triangles to find enough equations for  $ s,a,$  and  $ x$  that  you can solve them together. I'll let you fill in the details.</details>

