Find all pairs of real numbers  $(x,y)$  satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{align*}\frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{2y} &= (x^2+3y^2)(3x^2+y^2)
\frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{2y} &= 2(y^4-x^4)\end{align*}

candidates:
The only pair is  $$ \left(\frac{3^{1/5}+1}{2},\frac{3^{1/5}-1}{2}\right). $$  We start with a lemma.

<u>Lemma.</u> Given  $a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{R}$ , satisfying the system of equations
\begin{align*}
a&=b, 
c&=d,
\end{align*}
is logically equivalent to satisfying the system of equations
\begin{align*}
a+c&=b+d, 
a-c&=b-d.
\end{align*}
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>To start, the forward direction. The truth of an equation implies the truth of the equation with a given number added to each side. Since  $c=d$ , we may add  $c$  to  $a$  and  $d$  to  $b$  to arrive at  $a+c=b+d$  from  $a=b$ . Similarly, since  $-c=-d$ , we may add  $-c$  to  $a$  and  $-d$  to  $b$  to arrive at  $a-c=b-d$  from  $a=b$ .

To end, the backward direction. Since  $a-c=b-d$ , we may add  $a-c$  to  $a+c$  and  $b-d$  to  $b+d$  to arrive at  $2a=2b$  from  $a+c=b+d$ . Then, we may divide both sides by 2 to get  $a=b$ . Similarly, since  $-(a-c)=-(b-d)$ , we may add  $-(a-c)$  to  $a+c$  and  $-(b-d)$  to  $b+d$  to arrive at  $2c=2d$  from  $a+c=b+d$ . Then, we may divide both sides by 2 to get  $c=d$ .  $\square$</details>

Since division is not defined for zero, note that both  $x$  and  $y$  are nonzero.

By our lemma, the constraints are equivalent to
\begin{align*}
\frac2x&=x^4+10x^2y^2+5y^4,
\frac1y&=5x^4+10x^2y^2+y^4.
\end{align*}
Since  $x$  and  $y$  are both nonzero, we can multiply both sides of the first equation by  $x$  and both sides of the second equation by  $y$  to get that these constraints are equivalent to
\begin{align*}
2&=x^5+10x^3y^2+5xy^4,
1&=5x^4y+10x^2y^3+y^5.
\end{align*}
By our lemma again, these constraints are equivalent to
\begin{align*}
3&=(x+y)^5, 
1&=(x-y)^5.
\end{align*}
Take the fifth root (odd roots are reversible for real numbers) of both sides of each equation to get that these constraints are equivalent to 
\begin{align*}
3^{1/5}&=x+y, 
1&=x-y.
\end{align*}
By our lemma yet again, these constraints are equivalent to
\begin{align*}
3^{1/5}+1&=2x, 
3^{1/5}-1&=2y.
\end{align*}
Dividing both sides of both equations by 2, we get that these constraints are equivalent to
\begin{align*}
\frac{3^{1/5}+1}2&=x, 
\frac{3^{1/5}-1}2&=y,
\end{align*}
as desired.

