In all of this, a rare chance for an analyst to sink his teeth into a problem. Note that since  $f$  and  $g$  are continuous on a closed bounded interval and positive, the Extreme Value Theorem assures us that  $f,g,1/f,$  and  $1/g$  are all bounded functions on  $[a,b].$  Define  $A$  to be the subset of  $[a,b]$  on which  $f>g$  and let  $B$  be the complement: the subet of  $[a,b]$  on which  $f\le g.$  Note that  $\int_A(f-g)=\int_b(g-f)$  since overall,  $\int_a^b(f-g)=0.$  We also note that  $A$  must be nonempty (and relatively open in  $[a,b]$ ), since it it were empty, we would have to have  $f=g$  everywhere on  $[a,b],$  and that is denied by hypothesis. (In other words, we must have  $f>g$  some of the time and  $f<g$  some of the time.)
\begin{align*}I_{n+1}-I_n&=\int_a^b\frac{f^{n+2}}{g^{n+1}}-\frac{f^{n+1}}{g^n}
&=\int_a^b\left(\frac fg\right)^{n+1}(f-g)
&=\int_A\left(\frac fg\right)^{n+1}(f-g)-\int_B\left(\frac fg\right)^{n+1}(g-f)\end{align*}
On  $A,$   $\frac fg>1,$  hence  $\int_A\left(\frac fg\right)^{n+1}(f-g)>\int_A(f-g),$  and on  $B,$   $0<\frac fg\le 1,$  hence  $\int_B\left(\frac fg\right)^{n+1}(g-f)\le\int_B.$  Hence,  $I_{n+2}-I_n>\int_A(f-g)-\int_B(g-f)=0,$  which makes  $(I_n)$  an increasing sequence. Let  $x_0\in A.$  We have  $\frac{f(x_0)}{g(x_0)}=1+r$  for some  $r>0$  and  $f(x_0)=s>0.$  Since both  $f/g$  and  $f$  are continuous, there exists  $\delta_0$  such that for  $|x-x_0|<\delta,$   $\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}>1+\frac r2$  and  $f(x)>\frac s2.$  Then,
\[I_n=\int_a^b\left(\frac fg\right)^nf\ge\int_{|x-x_0|<\delta}\left(\frac fg\right)^nf>\int_{|x-x_0|<\delta}\left(1+\frac r2\right)^n\frac s2\ge\frac{s\delta}2\left(1+\frac r2\right)^n\] and that tends to  $\infty$  as  $n\to\infty,$  since  $r,$   $s$  and  $\delta$  are fixed.

