\]

Because the harmonic series diverges, there will be \(n > M\) such that \(F_n < 0\). But then it will also be \(E_n < 0\) and \(D_n < 0\), which is out of place.

So \(E_n > 0\) for every \(n > N\). (If we have \(E_n = 0\) for some \(n > N\), then \(E_{n+1} < 0\), out of place.) Then \(F_n\) is strictly ascending for \(n > M\). If at some stage \(F_n\) becomes non-negative, then it will also become positive, and as above, we can show that \(D_n\) will also become positive. So we can assume that \(F_n < 0\) for every \(n > N\). Then \(D_n + E_n + F_n < 0 + D_N\) for every \(n > N\). So \(E_n > -D_N > 0\) for every \(n > N\). Again, as above, however, we conclude that \(F_n\) will at some stage become positive.

So for \(n > N\), there is \(n\) such that \(D_n > 0\). If \(F_n \leqslant 0\), then we're done. Else \(D_{n+1} > D_n\). If \(F_{n+1} \leqslant 0\), we're done. Otherwise, \(D_{n+2} > D_{n+1}\), and so on; we can therefore assume that \(D_n\) becomes positive and ascending. Then the same will happen for \(E_n\) using \(F_n\) again, that the harmonic order diverges. But then we will have that all sequences are positive, out of place.

So it turned out to be the case.

