Suppose the lines intersect at point  $Q,$  and suppose  $\vec{u}$  is a vector in the direction of  $L_1$  and  $\vec{v}$  is a vector in the direction of  $L_2.$  Since the lines intersect, the direction vectors  $\vec{u}$  and  $\vec{v}$  must be linearly independent, and must span  $\mathbb{R}^2.$  Suppose  $\lambda$  is an arbitrary nonzero real number and suppose  $P$  is an arbitrary point in the plane. We can write  $\overrightarrow{QP}=\alpha \vec{u}+\beta\vec{v}.$  Then if  $A_1$  and  $A_2$  are arbitrary points on  $L_1,L_2,$  we may write  $\overrightarrow{QA_1}=x\vec{u}$  and  $\overrightarrow{QA_2}=y\vec{v}.$  Let us calculate what we want:
\begin{align*}\overrightarrow{PA_1}&=(\alpha-x)\vec{u}+\beta\vec{v}
\overrightarrow{PA_2}&=\alpha\vec{u}+(\beta-y)\vec{v}
\overrightarrow{PA_1}&=\lambda\overrightarrow{PA_2}
(\alpha-x)\vec{u}+\beta\vec{v}&=\lambda(\alpha\vec{u}+(\beta-y)\vec{v})
\end{align*}
Using independence, we match the coefficients of  $\vec{u}$  and  $\vec{v}.$  We have  $\alpha-x=\lambda\alpha,$  so  $x=(1-\lambda)\alpha,$  and  $\beta=\lambda(\beta-y),$  so  $y=\left(\frac{\lambda-1}\lambda\right)\beta.$ As long as  $\lambda\ne 0$ , we can solve for  $x$  and  $y$  and hence find points  $A_1$  and  $A_2$  that meet the condition. (The case  $\lambda=1$  looks a little unusual, but all that means is that  $A_1$  and  $A_2$  are both the point  $Q.$  It is a solution.)

Conversely, assume the lines do not intersect, which means that they are parallel. Choose  $P$  in the space between the lines. In order for  $\overrightarrow{PA_1}$  and  $\overrightarrow{PA_2}$  to be scalar multiples, they must lie on the same line - a line that crosses the parallel lines. We can see then that the ratio of  $\overrightarrow{PA_1}$  and  $\overrightarrow{PA_2}$  must be negative - in fact, a very specific negative number. If  $\lambda$  is positive, then we cannot solve this for  $A_1$  and  $A_2.$

