We repeatedly use the well-known fact that if  $2^m - 1 \mid 2^n - 1$ , then  $m \mid n$ . 

From  $n \mid 2^n$ , we have that  $n$  must be a power of  $2$ . Write  $n = 2^i$  for some nonnegative integer  $i$ . The second divisibility relation becomes
	\begin{align*}
		2^i - 1 &\mid 2^{2^i} - 1.
	\end{align*}
This implies that  $i \mid 2^i$ , so  $i$  must be a power of  $2$ . Write  $i = 2^j$  for some nonnegative integer  $j$ . The third divisibility relation becomes
	\begin{align*}
		2^{2^j} - 2 &\mid 2^{2^{2^j}} - 2 
		2^{2^j - 1} - 1 &\mid 2^{2^{2^j} - 1} - 1.
	\end{align*}
This implies that
	\begin{align*}
		2^j - 1 &\mid 2^{2^j} - 1,
	\end{align*}
which in turn implies that  $j \mid 2^j$ , so  $j$  must be a power of  $2$ . Write  $j = 2^k$  for some nonnegative integer  $k$ . Hence, we have
	\begin{align*}
		n &= 2^{2^{2^k}}.
	\end{align*}
Since  $n < 10^{100}$ , we have
	\begin{align*}
		2^{2^{2^k}} &< 10^{100} 
		2^{2^k} &< 100\log_{2}10 
		&< 400.
	\end{align*}
This implies that  $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$ . These respectively yield the solutions  $\boxed{n = 4, 16, 65536, 2^{256}}$ .

