Let  $R_n(x) = \sum_{i = 0}^n F_{i+1} {x \choose i}$ , and let  $S_n(x) = \sum_{i = 0}^n F_{i} {x \choose i}$ . The basic idea is to prove that  $R_n(k) = F_{2k+1}$  for all integers  $0 \leq k \leq n$ , but doing so by induction, we also need the fact that  $S_n(k) = F_{2k}$  for all  $0 \leq k \leq n$ . This is obvious for  $n = 0, 1$ . For the induction hypothesis, suppose that for al  $0 \leq k \leq n$ , we have  $R_n(k) = F_{2k+1}$  and  $S_n(k) = F_{2k}$ . We calculate  $R_{n+1}(k)$  and  $S_{n+1}(k)$  for all  $0 \leq k \leq n + 1$ .

For  $0 \leq k \leq n$ , easy to check that  $R_{n+1}(k) = R_n(k) = F_{2k+1}$  and  $S_{n+1}(k) = S_n(k) = F_{2k}$ , since  ${k \choose n+1} = 0$ . For  $k = n + 1$ ,
\begin{align*}
S_{n+1}(n+1) &= \sum_{i = 0}^{n+1} F_{i} {n+1 \choose i} 
&= \sum_{i = 1}^{n+1} F_{i} \left( {n \choose i} + {n \choose i-1}\right) 
&= \sum_{i = 1}^{n+1} F_{i} {n \choose i} + \sum_{i = 1}^{n+1} F_{i} {n \choose i-1} 
&= \sum_{i = 0}^{n} F_{i} {n \choose i} + \sum_{i = 0}^{n} F_{i+1} {n \choose i} 
&= R_n(n) + S_n(n) 
&= F_{2n+2} 
R_{n+1}(n+1) &= \sum_{i = 0}^{n+1} F_{i+1} {n+1 \choose i} 
&= \sum_{i = 0}^{n+1} (F_{i} + F_{i-1}) {n+1 \choose i} 
&= S_{n+1}(n+1) + 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^{n+1} F_{i-1} {n+1 \choose i} 
&= S_{n+1}(n+1) + 1 + \sum_{i = 0}^{n} F_{i} \left({n \choose i} + {n \choose i+1} \right) 
&= S_{n+1}(n+1) + 1 + R_n(n) + (S_n(n) - 1) 
&= F_{2n+3}
\end{align*}

Now, see that  $p(2x+1) = R_m(x)$  as a polynomial since they have the same degree  $m$  and their values match for  $m+1$  values of  $x$ . Next,  $$  R_m(m+1) = R_{m+1}(m+1) - F_{m+2} = F_{2m+3} - F_{m+2}  $$  Finally, we can prove that  $(j, k) = (2m + 3, m + 2)$  is unique through some quick bounding.</details>

