The Answer is  $\boxed{2^{2k}}$ We only need to show that  $$  \sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}}=1  $$  We consider the generating function  $G(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}} x^k$ We take  $t=k+j$ , and we have

\begin{align*}G(x)&=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}} x^k
&=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{t=k}^{2k} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k\end{align*}

Now, we consider another generating function,   $H(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{t=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k$ **We will show that  $H(x)=\frac{c}{1-x}$  for some constant  $c$ .**
*proof.*
In fact, for each  $k\ge 0$ , note

\begin{align*}
\sum_{t=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}}&=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=2k+1}^{\infty} \binom{t}{k} \cdot \sum_{i=t}^{\infty}\frac{1}{2^i}
&=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\sum_{t=2k+1}^i \binom{t}{k}}{2^i}
&=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{i+1}{k+1}-\binom{2k+1}{k+1}}{2^i}
&=\left( \sum_{i=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{i+1}{k+1}}{2^{i+1}}\right)-\binom{2k+1}{k+1}\cdot \sum_{i=2k+1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{2^i}
&=\sum_{i=2(k+1)}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{i+1}{k+1}}{2^{i+1}}+\binom{2k+1}{k+1}-\binom{2k+1}{k+1}
&=\sum_{t=2(k+1)+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{t}{k+1}}{2^{t}}
\end{align*}

Hence, we know that the coefficient for  $x^k$  and  $x^{k+1}$  are the same. Now, as the coefficients for each  $x^k$  is a constant, let  $c$  denote the constant, and we must have $ H(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} cx^k=\frac{c}{1-x}$ .
 口**Now, We evaluate**  $G(x)+H(x)$ \begin{align*} G(x)+H(x)&=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{t=k}^{2k} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k+\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{t=2k+1}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k
&=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{t=k}^{\infty} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k
&=\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \sum_{k=0 }^{t} \frac{\binom{t}{k}}{2^{t}} x^k
&=\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \frac{(x+1)^t}{2^t}
&=\frac{1}{1-\frac{x+1}{2}} &=\frac{2}{1-x} \end{align*}

Consequently, we know that  $$ \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}} x^k=G(x)=\frac{2-c}{1-x}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (2-c) x^k $$  Hence, note that when  $k=0$ ,  $\sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}}=1$ , we must have  $2-c=1$ , and finally

\begin{align*} \sum_{j=0}^k \frac{\binom{k+j}{k}}{2^{k+j}}=1 \end{align*}

We are done!

