I'm too lazy to write the full proof, but this is 差不多. The key observation is that
\begin{align*}
d(2p\cdot 2^n + q) \equiv d\big((2p+1)\cdot 2^n + q \big)\pmod{2}
\implies (-1)^{d(2p\cdot 2^n + q)} = -(-1)^{d((2p+1)\cdot 2^n + q)}
\end{align*}
for  $0\le q < 2^n$ , or more concretely, <details><summary>this.</summary>Basically, consider the following partitions of the signs of  $(-1)^{d(n)}$  from  $0$  to  $15$ .  $$ +-,-+,-+,+-,-+,+-,+-,-+ $$    $$ +--+,-++-,-++-,+--+ $$    $$ +--+-++-,-++-+--+ $$  The observation above essentially states that if we partition this sequence into segments of length  $2^{n+1}$ , then the first  $2^n$  signs in each segment will be opposite the last  $2^n$  signs.</details> Now, note that if  $n\equiv 0\pmod{2}$ , then  $$ \sum_{m=n}^{n + 1} (-1)^{d(m)}m^3 = \pm n^3 \mp (n+1)^3 = \mp f_1(n) $$  for some quadratic function  $f_1$ ; if  $n\equiv 0\pmod{4}$ , then  $$ \sum_{m=n}^{n + 3} (-1)^{d(m)}m^3 = \pm f_1(n) \mp f_1(n+2) = \mp f_2(n) $$  for some linear function  $f_2$ ; and so on, until we get   $$ \sum_{m=n}^{n + 15} (-1)^{d(m)}m^3 = 0 $$  for  $n\equiv 0\pmod{16}$ . Thus,  $$ S = (-1)^{d(2016)}\cdot 2016^3 + \ldots + (-1)^{2020}\cdot 2020^3, $$  so our desired answer is  $\boxed{1990}$ .

