Yay the putnams are out! <details><summary>Solution</summary>The key here is to take the log, use L'Hopital and substitute the end limit into  $e^L$  to finish.  It's an elementary solution but it arrives to the solution the fastest.
\begin{align*}
g(x)=\lim_{r\to 0}  ((x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1})^{\frac{1}{r}}&=\lim_{r\to 0} \frac{\log((x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1})}{r}
&=\lim_{r\to 0}\frac{-x^{r+1}\log(x) + (1 + x)^{r+1}\log(1 + x)}{-x^{r+1} + (1 + x)^{r + 1 }}
&=\frac{-x\log(x) + (1 + x)\log(1 + x)}{-x + (1 + x)}
&=(x + 1)\log(x + 1) - x\log(x)
\end{align*}
Hence our first half of the problem concludes that  $$ \lim_{r\to0}g(x)=e^{(x + 1)\log(x + 1) - x\log(x)}. $$  Now it suffices to find the value of   $$ \lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{g(x)}{x}\implies \lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{e^{(x + 1)\log(x + 1) - x\log(x)}}{x}. $$  Using L'Hopital finishes and is quickest.  We get
\begin{align*}
\lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{e^{(x + 1)\log(x + 1) - x\log(x)}}{x}&=\lim_{x\to\infty}x^{-x}(1 + x)^{x+1} (\log(1 + x)-\log(x))
&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(\frac{1}{x} + 1\right)^x (x + 1)\log\left(\frac{1}{x} + 1\right)
&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(\frac{1}{x} + 1\right)^x \cdot \lim_{x\to\infty}(x + 1)\log\left(\frac{1}{x} + 1\right)
&=e\cdot 1
&=\boxed{e}
\end{align*}</details>

\begin{align*}
\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{g(x)}x&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1x\lim_{r\to0}((x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1})^{\frac1r}
&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1x\lim_{r\to0}e^{\frac1r\ln((x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1})}
&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1xe^{\lim_{r\to0}\frac1r\ln((x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1})}
&=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1xe^{\lim_{r\to0}\frac{(x+1)^{r+1}\ln(x+1)-x^{r+1}\ln x}{(x+1)^{r+1}-x^{r+1}}}
