Solved with **kamatadu**. We had to take a few hints along the way.

We swap  $x$  and  $y$  and then add the two expressions to get
\[2I(R)=\iint\limits_{x^2 + y^2 = R^2} \mathrm dx \mathrm dy \left( \frac{2+2x^2+2y^2}{1+x^4+6x^2y^2+y^4}-\frac{2+x^2+y^2}{2+x^4+y^4} \right).\]

Now we swap to polar coordinates by using the substitution  $(x,y)=(r\cos\theta, r\sin\theta)$ . This gives us,
\[2I(R)=\int_{0}^{R}\int_{0}^{2\pi} r\mathrm dr\mathrm d\theta \left( \frac{2+2r^2}{1+r^4(1 + \sin^2(2\theta))}-\frac{2+r^2}{2+r^4(1-(1/2)\sin^2{2\theta})} \right).\]

Now we can substitute  $u=r^2$  to get,
\begin{align*}
    4I(R) &= \int_{0}^{R^2} \int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm du\mathrm{d}\theta \left( \frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(1 + \sin^2(2\theta))}-\frac{2+u}{2+u^2(1-(1/2)\sin^2{2\theta})} \right) 
    &= \int_{0}^{R^2} \int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm du\mathrm{d}\theta \left( \frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(1 + \sin^2(2\theta))}-\frac{4+2u}{4+u^2(2-\sin^2({2\theta}))} \right) 
    &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{0}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du \left(\frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(2-\sin^22\theta)}-\frac{4+2u}{4+u^2(2-\sin^2 2\theta)}\right) 
    &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{0}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du \left(\frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(2-\sin^22\theta)}\right)-\int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{0}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du\left(\frac{4+2u}{4+u^2(2-\sin^2 2\theta)}\right).
\end{align*}

We substitute  $u=2k$  in the last line to get,
\begin{align*}
    4I(R) &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{0}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du \left(\frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(2-\sin^22\theta)}\right)-\int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{0}^{R^2/2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm dk\left(\frac{2+2k}{1+k^2(2-\sin^2 2\theta)}\right) 
    &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{R^2/2}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du \frac{2+2u}{1+u^2(1+\cos^2(2\theta))}
\end{align*}

This gives us,
\[ 2I(R)=\int_{0}^{2\pi} \int_{R^2/2}^{R^2} \mathrm d\theta \mathrm du \frac{1+u}{1+u^2(1+\cos^2(2\theta))}.\]

Now we substitute  $u=vR^2$ .
\begin{align*}
    2I(R) &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm{d}\theta \int_{1/2}^{1} R^2 \mathrm dv \frac{1+R^2 v}{1+R^4v^2(1+\cos^2(2\theta))} 
    &= \int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm{d}\theta \int_{1/2}^{1} \mathrm dv \frac{1/R^2+ v}{1/R^4+v^2(1+\cos^2(2\theta))}.
\end{align*}

Let  $\lim\limits_{R\rightarrow \infty}I(R)=I$ .

So,
\begin{align*}
    I&=\int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm{d}\theta \int^{1}_{1/2} \mathrm{d}v\frac{1}{2v(1+\cos^2(2\theta))} 
    &=\frac{\ln 2}{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}\mathrm{d}\theta \frac{1}{1+\cos^2({2\theta})} 
    &= \ln 2\int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm{d}\theta \frac{1}{3+\cos (4\theta)}.
\end{align*}

Let  $\phi = 4\theta$ .

\[ I=\ln 2\int_{0}^{2\pi} \mathrm{d}\phi \frac{1}{3+\cos{\phi}}. \]

We convert  $I$  into a loop integral about the unit circle:
\[ I=2\ln 2 \oint -i\frac{\mathrm dz}{z}\frac{1}{6+(z+\frac{1}{z})}=2\ln 2 \oint -i\mathrm dz \frac{1}{z^2+6z+1}. \]

The only pole in the unit circle is  $-3+\sqrt8$ . Hence, on applying Cauchy Residue Theorem:
\[ I=(2\ln 2)(2\pi) \lim_{z\rightarrow -3+\sqrt8}\frac{1}{z+3+\sqrt8} =\boxed{\frac{\pi \ln 2}{\sqrt 2}}. \]

