The center of the square must be at least  $0.5$  away from each of these lines, so the region the center can be in is a square of side length  $\sec \theta - 1$ .  Thus, the answer is
\begin{align*}
\frac{4}{\pi} \int_0^{\pi / 4} (\sec \theta - 1)^2 d\theta &= \frac{4}{\pi} \int_0^{\pi / 4} \sec^2 \theta - 2\sec\theta + 1 d\theta
