The basics first:  Let  $X_0 \sim \text{Unif}[0,1]$  and define  $X_n$  by trimming a list of three "i.i.d  $X_{n-1}$ 's", so that  $X = X_{2021}$ .  Let  $F_n$  be the CDF of  $X_n$ .  Some <details><summary>casework</summary>Let the three i.i.d. "copies" of  $X_{n-1}$  be  $A,B,C$ .  If the median is  $\leq t$ , then there are two ways this can happen.**Case 1:** Two are below  $t$ , one is above  $t$ .

The probability of this is  $3\mathbb{P}(A \leq t,B \leq t, C > t)$  since there are three different ways to choose which variable is above  $t$ .  This is just  $3F_{n-1}(t)^2(1-F_{n-1}(t))$ .**Case 2:** All three are below  $t$ .

