First I show  $A$  can always have  $\ge 290$  cells open. The strategy for  $A$  is on each move, scan left to right and the first time he can place a tile after a 1-gap (irrespective of after a  $A$ -tile or a  $B$ -tile), do it. Keep doing this till no such moves are possible. At the end of this process, there can be no 3-gaps, so only 1-gaps and 2-gaps remain. After this, the remaining 2-gaps will all be filled, but we analyze what happens before this. 

There are  $A$ -tiles,  $B$ -tiles, 1-gaps, and 2-gaps. Say there are  $N_{A}, N_{B}, N_1, N_2$  of these respectively. Then 
\begin{align*}
N_A &\ge N_B \qquad \text{( $A$  goes first)} 
N_A &\le N_1 \qquad \text{(each  $A$ -tile comes after a 1-gap)} 
N_2 &\le N_B+1 \qquad \text{(2-gaps only created by placing of subsequent  $B$ -tile, or end)} 
2N_A &+ 2N_B + 2N_2 + N_1 = 2022.
\end{align*}
So  $N_1+1 \ge N_A+1 \ge N_B+1 \ge N_2$ , so  $7(N_1+1) \ge 2027$ , so  $N_1 \ge 288.5$ . But  $N_1$  even by parity, so  $N_1 \ge 290$ , as claimed. 
------------
Next I show  $B$  can always play so that  $\le 290$  cells are open. The strategy for  $B$  is on each move, scan left to right and the first time he can place a tile after a 2-gap (irrespective of after a  $A$ -tile or a  $B$ -tile), do it. Keep doing this till no such moves are possible. At the end of this process, there can be no 4-gaps, so only 1-gaps and 2-gaps and 3-gaps remain. 

After this checkpoint, the 3-gaps will all turn into 1-gaps, so let's analyze what happens once this happens. There are some 1-gaps and 2-gaps remaining. Let  $N_3$  be the number of 3-gaps that turned into 1-gaps, post-checkpoint. There are  $A$ -tiles,  $B$ -tiles, original 1-gaps (i.e. not 3-gaps turned into 1-gaps, post-checkpoint), and 2-gaps. Say there are  $N_{A}, N_{B}, N_1, N_2$  of these respectively. Then 
\begin{align*}
N_B + 1&\ge N_A \qquad \text{( $A$ ,  $B$  alternate)} 
N_1+N_3 &\le N_A+1 \qquad \text{(1-gap or 3-gap only created by placing of subsequent  $A$ -tile)} 
N_2 &\ge N_B \qquad \text{(each  $B$ -tile comes after a 2-gap)} 
2N_A &+ 2N_B + 2N_2 + (N_1+N_3) = 2022.
\end{align*}
