Therefore, it remains to show that  $n$  cannot be greater than or equal to  $5.$  

I prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** No three edges of the same color can be incident to the same vertex. 
*Proof.* Suppose for the sake of contradiction that some vertex  $v$  is adjacent to three other vertices  $v_1, v_2,$  and  $v_3$  such that the edges  $vv_1, vv_2,$  and  $vv_3$  are all colored blue. Then, if either of  $v_1v_2$  or  $v_1v_3$  is colored blue, then there will be a monochromatic blue triangle. Therefore, they must both not be blue. 

Also, if  $v_1v_2$  and  $v_1v_3$  are colored differently, then there will be three differently colored edges all incident to  $v_1,$  so they must actually be colored the same, say red. However, this means that  $v_2v_3$  can be neither blue nor red (or else there will be a monochromatic triangle), so it must be green, implying that  $v_3v, v_3v_1,$  and  $v_3v_2$  are differently colored, contradiction. 

Hence, our original supposition that there exist three edges of the same color incident to the same vertex must have been false, concluding the proof of this lemma.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, if  $n \ge 6,$  then every vertex will have a degree of  $5.$  Therefore, since no three differently colored edges can be incident to the same vertex, the Pigeonhole Principle tells us that every vertex is incident to   $$ \left\lceil \frac{5}{2} \right\rceil = 3 $$   edges of the same color. This is a contradiction by the lemma. Thus, it remains to consider the case when  $n=5.$ By the lemma, no three edges colored the same can be incident to the same vertex, so every vertex must be incident to two edges of one color and two edges of another color. Also, if some vertex is incident to two edges of some color, say blue, then at least three other vertices of the graph must also be incident to two blue edges in order for there not to be a monochromatic blue triangle. However, since every color must appear, it follows that there must be at least   $$ 4 \cdot 3 = 12 $$   edges, contradiction, because  $K_5$  has only  $\tbinom{5}{2}=10$  edges. 

Hence, since I have shown that  $n$  cannot be  $5$  or greater and I have provided an example graph for  $n=4,$  I have shown that the largest possible number of communities in the county is indeed  $4.$   $\square$

