\frac{m}{n} &= \frac{\overline{ab\cdots k}}{10^x} + \frac{\overline{pq\cdots u}}{10^x \cdot (10^y - 1)}
&= \frac{(10^y-1)\cdot \overline{ab\cdots k} + \overline{pq\cdots u}}{10^x \cdot (10^y-1)}.
\end{align*}
Consider the numerator modulo  $10$ . Note that  $(10^y-1)\cdot \overline{ab\cdots k} + \overline{pq\cdots u}\equiv u - k\not\equiv 0\pmod{10}$ . Thus, when simplified, the  $10^x$  will still have either a  $2$  or  $5$  factor left.

