I claim that the answer is  $x_0 \in (0, 1) \cap \mathbb{Q}.$  

Firstly, suppose that  $x_0$  is irrational. However, it is then evident that  $\{p_1/x_0\}$  must then be irrational as well or else  $x_0$  would be rational. Proceeding inductively in this way, we find that  $x_0, x_1, \ldots$  are all irrational, so the sequence cannot eventually become  $0.$  

Now, suppose that  $x_0$  is irrational. Note that if   $$ x_i = \frac{m}{n}, $$   for some relatively prime positive integers  $m$  and  $n$  and a nonnegative integer  $i,$  then we have
\begin{align*}
    x_{i+1} &= \left\{ \frac{p_{i+1}}{\tfrac{m}{n}}\right\} 
    &= \left\{\frac{np_{i+1}}{m}\right\}.
\end{align*}
This will have a denominator of at most  $m,$  which must be less than  $n$  because it is easy to see that all elements of the sequence  $x_0, \ldots, x_1$  must be in the range  $[0, 1).$  Therefore, the denominators of  $x_0, x_1, \ldots$  when expressed in simplest form are a strictly decreasing monovariant, implying that it must eventually be  $1.$  At this point, the fraction would have to be  $\tfrac{0}{1}=0,$  concluding the proof.  $\square$

